<div><br><strong>Ultrasound-guided barbotage (lavage) for calcific tendinitis</strong> involves percutaneous needle aspiration and irrigation of calcium deposits, typically using saline solution to fragment and extract hydroxyapatite crystals from the tendon. The procedure is most effective during the <strong>resorptive stage</strong> of calcific tendinopathy, when deposits are soft and unstable, though clinical outcomes vary based on multiple factors.<br><br><h2>Pathogenesis</h2><br><br>Calcific tendinopathy results from calcium hydroxyapatite crystal deposition within tendons through an active cell-mediated process.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38346707">[1]</a><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29195923">[2]</a><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22917025">[3]</a> The pathogenesis involves:<br><br>- <strong>Cellular transformation</strong>: Tenocytes differentiate into chondrocyte-like cells within fibrocartilage areas of the tendon, producing a pro-mineralizing environment<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29195923">[2]</a><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22917025">[3]</a><br><br>- <strong>Molecular pathways</strong>: Bone morphogenetic proteins (BMPs), Wnt signaling, and TGF-β pathways drive calcification and calcium imbalance<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39597785">[4]</a><br><br>- <strong>Inflammatory resorption</strong>: During spontaneous resorption, apatite crystals migrate into the subacromial bursa, activating the NLRP3 inflammasome and triggering IL-1β and IL-18 release, causing severe pain and inflammation<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29195923">[2]</a><br><br>The deposits contain poorly crystalline carbonated apatite with proteins, particularly osteopontin, which may regulate the process.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29195923">[2]</a><br><br><h2>Stages and Clinical Presentation</h2><br><br>The disease progresses through distinct phases, with the <strong>resorptive stage</strong> causing the most severe symptoms when calcium becomes soft and unstable.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38346707">[1]</a> During this phase, calcium may migrate to adjacent tissues including the bursa, bone, or muscle.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38346707">[1]</a><br><br><strong>Ultrasound classification</strong> identifies three deposit types:<a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2022/1230857">[5]</a><br><br>- Hyperechoic focus with clear shadow (79%)<br><br>- Hyperechoic with dull shadow (14%)<br><br>- Hyperechoic without shadow (7%)<br><br><h2>Treatment Effects of Barbotage</h2><br><br>The evidence for barbotage effectiveness is <strong>mixed and controversial</strong>:<br><br><strong>Recent high-quality evidence</strong> from a large randomized controlled trial (220 patients) found <strong>no significant benefit</strong> for ultrasound-guided lavage with corticosteroid injection compared to sham treatment at 4 months (OSS difference 0.2, 95% CI -2.3 to 2.8, P=1.0) or at 24 months.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37821122">[6]</a> This pragmatic trial challenges the routine use of barbotage.<br><br>However, <strong>earlier systematic reviews</strong> suggested potential benefits:<br><br>- Meta-analysis comparing barbotage to extracorporeal shockwave therapy (ESWT) showed significant pain reduction favoring barbotage at 52 weeks (SMD=-0.52, 95% CI -0.85 to -0.19, P=0.002), though not at earlier timepoints<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39382530">[7]</a><br><br>- Another meta-analysis found barbotage superior to ESWT for pain relief and calcification clearance at 12 months<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31124934">[8]</a><br><br>- A recent systematic review concluded that certainty of evidence ranges from low to very low, with doubtful results compared to other minimally invasive approaches<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39382530">[7]</a><br><br><h2>Factors Associated With Better Outcomes</h2><br><br>Post-hoc analysis of randomized trial data identified specific predictors of success following barbotage:<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33719606">[9]</a><br><br><strong>Procedural factors</strong>:<br><br>- <strong>Calcium extraction during procedure</strong> (OR 10.7, 95% CI 1.791-63.927 at 6 months)<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33719606">[9]</a><br><br>- <strong>Creating communication between calcification and subacromial bursa</strong> (OR 2.728 at 3 months; OR 9.835 at 12 months for calcification disappearance)<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33719606">[9]</a><br><br>- <strong>Corticosteroid injection after procedure</strong> (OR 3.143, 95% CI 1.105-8.94 for good outcomes at 3 months)<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33719606">[9]</a><br><br><strong>Clinical indicators</strong>:<br><br>- Lower DASH score at 3 months predicts favorable 6-month outcomes (OR 0.92)<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33719606">[9]</a><br><br>- Lower DASH at 6 months predicts 12-month success (OR 0.939)<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33719606">[9]</a><br><br>- <strong>Calcification resorption correlates with good clinical outcomes</strong> at all timepoints<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33719606">[9]</a><br><br><strong>Ultrasound findings favoring success</strong>:<br><br>- Deposits in the resorptive phase (soft, less echogenic)<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38346707">[1]</a><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40806998">[10]</a><br><br>- Ability to visualize and access the deposit clearly<br><br>- Successful fragmentation with milky aspirate during procedure<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40806998">[10]</a><br><br><h2>Technical Approach</h2><br><br>The <strong>one-needle technique</strong> involves inserting a 16-18 gauge needle attached to a 20 mL syringe containing 0.9% saline solution into the calcific deposit under ultrasound guidance using an in-plane approach.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40806998">[10]</a> The plunger is repeatedly pressed and released until the aspirate becomes milky, indicating successful calcium extraction. The syringe is replaced as needed until adequate lavage is achieved.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40806998">[10]</a><br><br><strong>Post-procedure management</strong>: Physiotherapy typically begins 7 days after the procedure, consisting of home exercises to restore range of motion and function.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37821122">[6]</a><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40806998">[10]</a><br><br><h2>Clinical Implications</h2><br><br>Given the conflicting evidence, barbotage should be considered as <strong>one option among several</strong> for symptomatic calcific tendinopathy, particularly in the resorptive stage. The procedure appears safest when:<br><br>- Deposits are clearly visualized on ultrasound<br><br>- The resorptive phase is confirmed (softer, less echogenic deposits)<br><br>- Communication with the bursa can be established during the procedure<br><br>- Calcium extraction is achieved (milky aspirate)<br><br>Assessment at 3 months is essential for management strategy, as early clinical response predicts long-term outcomes.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33719606">[9]</a> The recent high-quality sham-controlled trial suggests that benefits may be less robust than previously thought, and shared decision-making with patients is warranted.<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37821122">[6]</a><br><br>Would you like to explore the specific ultrasound characteristics that differentiate the formative versus resorptive stages of calcific tendinopathy to optimize patient selection for barbotage?<br><br><h3>References</h3><ol type="1"><li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38346707">Imaging of Calcific Tendinopathy: Natural History, Migration Patterns, Pitfalls, and Management: A Review</a>. Saran S, Babhulkar JA, Gupta H, Chari B. The British Journal of Radiology. 2024;97(1158):1099-1111. doi:10.1093/bjr/tqae039.</li><li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29195923">Calcific Tendonitis of the Rotator Cuff: From Formation to Resorption</a>. Darrieutort-Laffite C, Blanchard F, Le Goff B. Joint Bone Spine. 2018;85(6):687-692. doi:10.1016/j.jbspin.2017.10.004.</li><li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22917025">Physiopathology of Intratendinous Calcific Deposition</a>. Oliva F, Via AG, Maffulli N. BMC Medicine. 2012;10:95. doi:10.1186/1741-7015-10-95.</li><li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39597785">Exploring Metabolic Mechanisms in Calcific Tendinopathy and Shoulder Arthrofibrosis: Insights and Therapeutic Implications</a>. Alam S, Sargeant MS, Patel R, Jayaram P. Journal of Clinical Medicine. 2024;13(22):6641. doi:10.3390/jcm13226641.</li><li><a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2022/1230857">Effects of High‐Energy Extracorporeal Shockwave Therapy on Pain, Functional Disability, Quality of Life, and Ultrasonographic Changes in Patients with Calcified Rotator Cuff Tendinopathy</a>. Fatima A, Ahmad A, Gilani SA, et al. BioMed Research International. 2022;2022:1230857. doi:10.1155/2022/1230857.</li><li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37821122">Ultrasound Guided Lavage With Corticosteroid Injection Versus Sham Lavage With and Without Corticosteroid Injection for Calcific Tendinopathy of Shoulder: Randomised Double Blinded Multi-Arm Study</a>. Moosmayer S, Ekeberg OM, Hallgren HB, et al. BMJ (Clinical Research Ed.). 2023;383:e076447. doi:10.1136/bmj-2023-076447.</li><li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39382530">Ultrasound-Guided Percutaneous Lavage for the Treatment of Rotator Cuff Calcific Tendinopathy: A Systematic Review With Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials</a>. Sconza C, Palloni V, Lorusso D, et al. European Journal of Physical and Rehabilitation Medicine. 2024;60(6):995-1008. doi:10.23736/S1973-9087.24.08544-7.</li><li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31124934